Step 1: Secure Streaming & Ingestion (The DevSecOps Layer)
The Data: Write a Python script to simulate continuous, highly sensitive streaming transactional financial data containing PII 
(names, emails, card digits).
In Transit: Stream this data into Apache Kafka (deployed on Kubernetes) or AWS Kinesis.
Your Edge: Secure the broker using TLS encryption, implement SASL/SCRAM authentication, and implement an automated schema validation check using a Schema 
Registry. If a poison pill message with a broken schema enters the stream, catch it via a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ).


Step 2: Real-Time Masking & Ingestion (The Data Engineering Layer)
The Stream Processor: Build an Apache Spark Streaming application (using PySpark) that consumes from your stream.
Your Edge: Use Spark to dynamically parse the JSON payload, strip or mask the PII data (e.g., hashing emails or obfuscating credit card numbers using 
encryption keys retrieved from HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager), and append the sanitized data into your storage layer.


Step 3: Storage Optimization via Lakehouse (The Modern Storage Layer)
The Destination: Write the processed stream directly into an Apache Iceberg table format sitting on an object store (like AWS S3 or MinIO).
Your Edge: Configure the Iceberg table with partitioned storage based on the transaction date. Implement standard data management practices like automated 
retention policies, snapshot isolation, and time-travel querying.


Step 4: Orchestrated Batch Transformations (The Analytical Layer)

The Modeler: Use dbt (Data Build Tool) to build your analytical batch models over the Iceberg table data. Aggregate metrics like Hourly Transaction 
Volumes and Fraud Risk Flags.
The Conductor: Orchestrate the entire batch layer using Apache Airflow. Write an Airflow DAG that runs every night to optimize the Iceberg table
(running data compaction / vacuuming to merge small files) and triggers the dbt transformation.

Your Edge: Integrate Great Expectations into your Airflow DAG to run data quality assertions 
(e.g., verifying transaction_amount is never negative) before writing to final production schemas.


Step 5: Automated Deployment & Monitoring (The Platform Layer
Infrastructure as Code (IaC): Write Terraform modules to provision your entire stack—S3 buckets, IAM roles, Kubernetes namespaces, 
and access keys.

CI/CD: Use GitHub Actions or GitLab CI to build a pipeline that runs unit tests on your Spark code, validates your dbt models, and automatically packages 
and deploys your Airflow DAGs when code changes are pushed to your repository.
